1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,990
In 1637, a single Dutch flower bulb could sell for as much as a house.

2
00:00:06,550 --> 00:00:09,690
Not gold. Not diamonds. A tulip.

3
00:00:10,150 --> 00:00:15,330
Tulips had come from the Ottoman Empire, and the Dutch fell in love with them.

4
00:00:15,850 --> 00:00:19,710
The rarest ones had flames of colour across their petals.

5
00:00:19,750 --> 00:00:23,530
And no one could predict which bulb would bloom like that.

6
00:00:23,950 --> 00:00:25,890
So people began to bet.

7
00:00:26,450 --> 00:00:31,530
They traded in taverns, through the winter, while the bulbs were still in the ground.

8
00:00:31,710 --> 00:00:34,130
Buying flowers nobody had seen.

9
00:00:34,650 --> 00:00:37,330
Prices doubled. Then doubled again.

10
00:00:37,410 --> 00:00:40,550
People bought, just to sell a week later, for more.

11
00:00:41,050 --> 00:00:46,730
The most famous bulb of all, the Semper Augustus, was said to be worth a mansion.

12
00:00:48,050 --> 00:00:54,490
And then, in February 1637, at a sale in Haarlem, the buyers stayed away.

13
00:00:55,050 --> 00:00:58,630
Within days, the panic spread. Prices collapsed.

14
00:00:59,090 --> 00:01:02,370
People refused to pay for bulbs they had promised to buy.

15
00:01:03,050 --> 00:01:07,050
For centuries, it was told as the story of a country ruined.

16
00:01:07,330 --> 00:01:09,630
But few people actually went broke.

17
00:01:09,690 --> 00:01:11,630
What really broke was trust.

18
00:01:12,750 --> 00:01:14,310
And the strangest part?

19
00:01:14,350 --> 00:01:17,670
Those flames on the petals were caused by a virus.

20
00:01:18,250 --> 00:01:21,570
The most valuable flowers in history were sick.

21
00:01:21,730 --> 00:01:24,710
And the sickness was what people paid for.

22
00:01:25,350 --> 00:01:30,570
A bubble begins when people stop buying the thing, and start buying the price.

